<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hauran</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hawran, Auranitis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hauran is a volcanic plateau and fertile plain in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, extending into northern Jordan, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a>. Long a granary for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, it was ruled in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaeans">Aramaeans</a>, Nabataeans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and Ghassanids, and its black basalt towns, such as Bosra, preserve many ancient buildings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hauran.jpg" alt="Hauran" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amitchell125 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Hauran_(satellite_version).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Hauran spans parts of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and northern Jordan. It is bounded in the north by the Ghouta oasis, to the west by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golan-heights">Golan Heights</a> and to the east and south by desert steppe. Traditionally it has three subregions: the Nuqrah and Jaydur plains, the Jabal Hauran massif, also called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze">Jabal al-Druze</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trachonitis">Lajat</a> volcanic field. The Nuqrah is a low plateau of fertile basalt soil. The plain receives an average 250 millimetres of rain a year, enough for grain, while the Jabal Hauran rises above 1,800 metres and receives more rain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Settlements in the region, then generally called Bashan, appear in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-deuteronomy">Book of Deuteronomy</a>. The Aramean kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-israel">Kingdom of Israel</a> contested it, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> held it from 732 to 610 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucids</a> took it from the Ptolemies after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-panium">Battle of Panium</a> in 200 BCE, and the Nabatean kingdom expanded to Bosra and Salkhad. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> extended its influence to all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> by 63 BCE and formally annexed the Hauran in the 2nd century CE.</p><p>The Hauran prospered under Roman rule and in the Byzantine era, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> relied on Arab tribes, among them the Salihids and Ghassanids, to guard it. Muslim forces arrived in April 634 and took Bosra in May, and after the Battle of Yarmouk in 636 the whole region came under Muslim rule. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1516. Under the French Mandatory period, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-syrian-revolt">Great Syrian Revolt</a> that began there, its plains formed a district of the State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and the Jabal Hauran the Jabal Druze State. The Hauran remained the main grain source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> until northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> overtook it in the mid-20th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The population is largely Arab and religiously mixed: Sunni Muslim agrarian clans on the plains, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> majority in the Jabal Hauran and Greek Orthodox and Greek Catholic communities in its western foothills. The largest towns are Daraa, Ramtha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwayda">Suwayda</a>. Because basalt was the common building stone, the Hauran has a distinct all-basalt architecture, and some 300 towns and villages contain ancient structures, among them Bosra, Qanawat, Shahba, Salkhad and Umm al-Jimal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hauran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Druze moved east to the <b>Hauran</b> in waves from 1685, and after the battle of Ain Dara in 1711 many Yamani Druze followed, laying the foundation of the Jabal al-Druze, while Maronites became the dominant group in Mount Lebanon.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>It tells that Nasoraean Mandaeans, disciples of John the Baptist, left Jerusalem in the first century CE, reportedly because of persecution, and went to Haran or <b>Hauran</b>, then to the Median hills in Iran, and finally settled in southern Mesopotamia.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mandaeism">Mandaeism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings ruled from Damascus over the oasis and the <b>Hauran</b>, controlling caravan routes from Arabia.</blockquote><a href="/state/aram-damascus">Aram-Damascus</a></li><li><blockquote>Duqaq's emirate covered Damascus, the <b>Hawran</b> and parts of the Beqaa.</blockquote><a href="/state/seljuk-emirate-of-damascus">Seljuk Emirate of Damascus</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip received the lands north and east of the Sea of Galilee: Gaulanitis, Batanaea, Trachonitis, <b>Auranitis</b> and part of Ituraea, a frontier zone the Romans had entrusted to Herod to suppress brigandage.</blockquote><a href="/state/tetrarchy-of-philip">Tetrarchy of Philip</a></li></ul></section>
